# Implement durable T3 routing state journal and singleton

## Context

Design: `.collab/design/2026-10-19-c2c-t3-external-connector.md`.
Pending planned work. During execution follow bl-fix-many: read the full ticket, verify blockers, commit the claim, work in an isolated worktree, review/fix, run the c2c gates on build boxes, merge and close. No T3 source changes. Tests use x-p35/x-tuf, never the workstation's full suite. Actual just bi and service installation wait for the current candidate's successful P9.M2.E1.T001 buildbox gate.

## Requirements

- Own C2c_t3_state and state tests. Persist stable environment identity, opaque t3-hash aliases, thread mappings, tombstones and a bounded inbound message journal.
- Key deliveries by source broker/identity + broker message ID + target thread/environment. Persist exact payload, request ID, mode, credential generation and result before exposing transitions.
- Define recoverable acceptance and selected broker ACK ordering with strict file/directory durability or an existing transactional store. Never drain before durable acceptance.
- Hold an OS singleton lock for the full service lifetime, keyed to canonical environment/home so alternate config spelling cannot create another owner. Multiple environments may each have one owner.
- Retain uncertainty on credential rotation and tombstones on retirement. Do not deduplicate intentional identical text with distinct message IDs.
- Store recovery intents in this journal, unique by environment/thread/failed run, with sorted error IDs/evidence fingerprint, provider/model, exact Continue payload, queue mode, request ID and credential generation. Additional errors within one failed run do not mint another recovery.
- Persist recovery outcomes, last per-thread recovery submission and the shared dispatcher submission boundary so service restart does not reset dedupe or permit a burst. Define schema migration before dependent service work.

## Acceptance Criteria

- Crash/fault tests cover every accept/ACK/send/receipt transition, concurrent starts, atomic writes, corrupt state, collision handling, restart, capacity/backpressure and ownership-preserving repair.
- A process crash releases the OS lock. No live lock inode is removed during cleanup.
- Retrying one delivery reuses its payload and request ID. Distinct broker IDs remain distinct deliveries.
- Pending mail is never deleted by retirement/repair. Retention and capacity rules are explicitly documented.

## Lane

Can run in parallel with T002 after T001. Own only state module/tests until the shared service integration.

Lane decision: parallel with T002 only after T001 merges. Own durable routing state and its tests; do not edit the MCP client.